{"id":"multi-layer-perceptron-forward-pass","t":"Forward pass of a configurable MLP","tags":["Neural Networks"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"mlp_forward","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"A multi-layer perceptron is a stack of affine maps and nonlinearities:\n\n$$z^{(k)} = a^{(k-1)} W_k + b_k, \\qquad a^{(k)} = f_k\\big(z^{(k)}\\big), \\qquad a^{(0)} = x.$$\n\nWrite `mlp_forward(x, layers)` that runs the network and returns `(out, cache)`.\n\n## Inputs\n- `x`: float array, either a single sample `(d,)` or a batch `(N, d)`.\n- `layers`: a list of `(W, b, act)` tuples. `W` has shape `(in, out)` (rows are inputs), `b` has shape `(out,)`, and `act` is one of `'relu'`, `'tanh'`, `'sigmoid'`, `'identity'`, `'softmax'`. Softmax is over the last axis and must be stable for large logits.\n\n## Output\n- `out`: the final activation $a^{(L)}$. A 1-D input gives a 1-D output `(out,)`; a 2-D input gives `(N, out)`.\n- `cache`: a list with one tuple `(z, a)` per layer, in order, holding that layer's pre-activation and activation, each shaped like `out` would be at that layer (1-D for a 1-D input). Backprop needs exactly these.\n- An empty `layers` list returns `(x as float, [])`.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef mlp_forward(x, layers):\n    \"\"\"layers: list of (W (in, out), b (out,), act). Return (out, [(z, a), ...]).\"\"\"\n    a = np.asarray(x, dtype=float)\n    cache = []\n    for W, b, act in layers:\n        z = W @ a + b                      # is this the right orientation?\n        a = np.maximum(z, 0.0)\n        cache.append((z, a))\n    return a, cache\n","hints":["With `W` stored as `(in, out)`, the layer is `a @ W + b`. For a 1-D `a` of shape `(in,)` that gives `(out,)` automatically; for `(N, in)` it gives `(N, out)`.","Write one helper that maps an activation name to a function. Sigmoid and softmax need the usual stability tricks (split on the sign; subtract the row max).","Store `(z, a)` after every layer. Note that the identity layer has `z` and `a` equal, and an empty network returns the input unchanged."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef _act(z, name):\n    if name == 'relu':\n        return np.maximum(z, 0.0)\n    if name == 'tanh':\n        return np.tanh(z)\n    if name == 'sigmoid':\n        e = np.exp(-np.abs(z))\n        return np.where(z >= 0, 1 / (1 + e), e / (1 + e))\n    if name == 'identity':\n        return z\n    if name == 'softmax':\n        e = np.exp(z - z.max(axis=-1, keepdims=True))\n        return e / e.sum(axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n    raise ValueError(name)\n\ndef mlp_forward(x, layers):\n    a = np.asarray(x, dtype=float)\n    cache = []\n    for W, b, act in layers:\n        z = a @ np.asarray(W, dtype=float) + b      # works for (d,) and (N, d) alike\n        a = _act(z, act)\n        cache.append((z, a))\n    return a, cache\n","ex_md":"## Row-vector convention\nStoring `W` as `(in, out)` and multiplying `a @ W` treats each sample as a row. A whole batch then goes through one matrix product, and the same code handles a single 1-D sample because NumPy's `@` treats a 1-D left operand as a row vector. The column convention $W a$ (PyTorch stores `(out, in)` and computes `x @ W.T`) is equivalent; what matters is being consistent.\n\n## Why keep the cache\nThe backward pass needs $f_k'(z^{(k)})$ and $a^{(k-1)}$ at every layer: $\\partial L/\\partial W_k = (a^{(k-1)})^\\top\\,\\delta^{(k)}$. Recomputing them is possible (activation checkpointing trades compute for memory), but by default frameworks store them, and that is what dominates training memory.\n\n## Complexity\n$\\sum_k N \\cdot n_{k-1} n_k$ multiply-adds; memory for the cache is $\\sum_k N n_k$.\n\n## Common bugs\n- `W @ a` with `(in, out)` weights: shapes only line up by accident when the layer is square.\n- An unstable softmax or sigmoid: test 5 feeds logits in the hundreds.\n- Caching `a` by reference and then modifying it in place on the next layer.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Count the parameters of a 784-256-128-10 MLP. (200,704 + 32,896 + 1,290 = 234,890.)\n- Why does stacking linear layers without nonlinearities add no expressive power?","tests":[{"a":[{"__nd__":[1.0,2.0],"dtype":"float64"},[{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[1.0,-1.0],[0.0,1.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[0.0,-2.0],"dtype":"float64"},"relu"]}]],"e":{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[1.0,0.0],"dtype":"float64"},[{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[1.0,-1.0],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[1.0,0.0],"dtype":"float64"}]}]]}},{"a":[{"__nd__":[[1.0],[-1.0]],"dtype":"float64"},[{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[2.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[0.0],"dtype":"float64"},"identity"]},{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[1.0,-1.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[0.0,0.0],"dtype":"float64"},"softmax"]}]],"e":{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[0.9820137900379085,0.017986209962091555],[0.017986209962091555,0.9820137900379085]],"dtype":"float64"},[{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[2.0],[-2.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[2.0],[-2.0]],"dtype":"float64"}]},{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[2.0,-2.0],[-2.0,2.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[0.9820137900379085,0.017986209962091555],[0.017986209962091555,0.9820137900379085]],"dtype":"float64"}]}]]}},{"a":[{"__nd__":[[-0.4763135491383076,1.8184817231498083,0.5195393601930505,0.04979744331439894],[-0.9018171912232378,0.3681847205816066,-0.48653003243104254,-0.2227691793707828],[-1.6225108173922826,-0.4905539302374794,0.15987903483797983,-1.250806160003097],[0.07389650956670456,0.6632938789578444,-0.3067599406971191,-0.6207841599596035],[-0.6201241876096135,0.9411423059808348,-1.5345050452989106,-0.30045139331057524]],"dtype":"float64"},[{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[-1.738266398496882,-1.3366427931811324,-1.361106708564987,-0.35161713127840977,-2.3125815796967033,-0.18889719608460778],[-0.957229228096346,0.8936001849299788,0.956847237579234,1.3922582291390866,0.7674701130947078,-0.053029778757267734],[0.8597939889439096,1.5054811563838433,-0.6535945334170485,0.610351145830187,-0.042673827710852374,1.4400167254152394],[-0.8368950200968434,-0.3015466095655266,0.3623385931694391